Upstream Hyprland 0.56.2 bug (traced locally on 2026-10-30, not yet
reported upstream): CGLElementRenderer::draw(WP<X> element, ...)
dereferences `element->m_data` with no validity check, for every pass
element type. IElementRenderer::drawElement()'s own `if (!element) return;`
guard only catches a never-assigned weak pointer -- hyprutils'
CWeakPointer::valid() (what `operator bool` calls) explicitly does NOT
catch "target object mid-destruction" the way expired() does (see
hyprutils' WeakPtr.hpp comment on valid() vs expired()). dynamicPointerCast
itself checks expired() correctly and returns a null WP<X> in that case,
but the draw() overloads below never check that null result before
dereferencing it.

In practice: a window's border/shadow/rect/etc. pass element gets queued
for the current frame, then the window is destroyed (closed, or an
XWayland/transient surface torn down) before that frame actually renders.
The queued element is now a dangling/expired weak pointer, and whichever
draw() overload handles it aborts (if CWeakPointer's internal assertion
catches it) or segfaults (if not) -- confirmed via ~20 matching crash
reports in ~/.cache/hyprland/, almost all firing seconds after Hyprland
starts, when autostart apps and restored browser windows are mapping (and
sometimes churning) in a burst. A prior workaround in
config/hypr/conf/input.lua (shadow.enabled = false) silenced the
CShadowPassElement instance of this same bug; this patch fixes it at the
root for every pass element type, including CBorderPassElement, which
that workaround didn't cover and which kept crashing on every boot.

